==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(s)teyg-}}
From the {{der|en|la|īnstīgātus}}, past participle of {{m|la|instigo|īnstīgāre|t=to instigate}}, from prefix {{m|la|in-|t=in}} + {{m|la||*stigare}}, akin to {{m|la|stinguo|stinguere|t=push, goad}}. Compare {{cog|de|stechen|t=to prick}}, {{cog|en|stick}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɪnstəɡeɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-instigate.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} to [[incite]]; to bring about by urging or encouraging
#: {{ux|en|to '''instigate''' a riot}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2017|author=Desa Markovic|title=Working with Sexual Issues in Psychotherapy
|passage=If the man perceives that his partner has arousal or orgasmic difficulties, this is likely to influence his desire to '''instigate''' sexual activity and/or his enjoyment and pleasure in being sexual with his partner.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} to [[goad]] or [[urge]] (a person) forward, especially to wicked actions; to [[provoke]]
#: {{ux|en|to '''instigate''' someone to a crime}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1678|author={{w|Robert Barclay}}|title=An Apology for the True Christian Divinity
|passage=he might '''instigate''' them to swear against the law of God}}
#* {{RQ:Warburton Divine Legation|passage=He hath only '''instigated''' his blackest agents to the very extent of their malignity.}}
#: {{syn|en|animate|encourage|impel|incite|provoke|spur|stimulate|tempt|urge}}
#: {{ant|en|halt|prevent|stop}}

